---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import FeedbackLinks from "../components/FeedbackLinks.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/sergiogallegos/OpenWebHMI";
const BLOB = `${REPO}/blob/main`;
---

<BaseLayout title="OpenWebHMI">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-inner">
      <Logo size={88} />
      <span class="badge warn">Pre-alpha · foundation hardening</span>
      <h1>Open-source SCADA/HMI for real machines.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Design operator views, run them in a browser, and connect plant-floor
        data through a Rust gateway. OpenWebHMI is a pre-alpha platform built
        in public for small and mid-size industrial systems.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/download">Build from source →</a>
        <a
          class="button secondary"
          href="https://github.com/sergiogallegos/OpenWebHMI"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >GitHub ↗</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-meta subtle">
        AGPL core · MPL protocol packages · no JVM · no per-server fees
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>Product path</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      A working SCADA/HMI foundation comes first. The reference demo and
      manufacturing features build on that foundation without expanding the
      v1.0 promise. Optional services connecting multiple sites are a later direction.
    </p>
    <div class="status-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="badge">Source available · pre-alpha</span>
        <h3>SCADA/HMI foundation</h3>
        <p>
          Source builds include the Rust gateway, browser runtime, form-based
          designer, tag flow, history, alarms, authentication, scripting, and
          a Rockwell-protocol simulator vertical slice.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="badge">In development</span>
        <h3>Default reference demo</h3>
        <p>
          One fictional manufacturing HMI will ship with deterministic offline
          simulation and an explicit real Rockwell PLC profile using the same
          screens and logical tags. It will never silently fall back between
          profiles.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="badge">Direction</span>
        <h3>Optional manufacturing modules</h3>
        <p>
          Production, downtime, OEE, reporting, and equipment modules are a
          post-1.0 direction—not shipped v1.0 features or an MES promise.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <span class="badge">Post-1.0 direction</span>
    <h2>Local operation. A future view across locations.</h2>
    <p>
      Start with a gateway and browser on one PC, or a local server serving
      clients across the plant. The future direction is independent gateways
      at multiple locations, with optional central services for authorized
      remote dashboards, selected history, analytics, reporting, and business
      logic across sites. Central services could be self-hosted or managed in
      the cloud.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Local HMI operation, alarms, and operational logic must continue without
      internet; PLCs retain machine control. Cloud connectivity will be opt-in.
      Data forwarding, multi-site dashboards, and fleet management are not
      available today and are outside the initial beta and v1.0 scope.
      <a href={`${BLOB}/docs/roadmap.md#edge--cloud-direction--accepted-2026-10-24`}>Read the future deployment direction →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <span class="badge">Planned for v1</span>
    <h2>Build visually. Bring your favorite coding agent.</h2>
    <p>
      Author your SCADA/HMI in the Designer, or use an external coding agent to
      help create screens, bindings, configuration, and Python scripts. The goal
      is one open project you can edit from either workflow, then preview and
      review in the browser Designer before publishing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your HMI application will have its own local project folder, ready for
      an optional Git repository. Use GitHub or another Git host to share branches,
      review changes as a team, and validate in CI. Coding agents and the visual
      Designer will work with the same documented source, validation tools, and
      preview. Pushing or merging source will not publish to a running gateway;
      deployment remains a separate authorized step.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      No required AI provider or AI subscription to use the Designer. Portable
      project files, terminal tools, team repository workflows, and conflict-aware
      editing are planned; the supported team and agent workflows are not
      available yet.
      <a href={`${BLOB}/docs/planning/agent-authoring.md`}>Read the authoring plan →</a>
      <a href={`${BLOB}/docs/planning/project-engineering.md#team-repositories-and-agent-friendly-collaboration`}>See the team workflow →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>Why another platform?</h2>
    <p>
      Industrial automation has been locked behind closed source tooling and
      expensive gateway licensing for decades. Tools like
      Inductive Automation Ignition and Rockwell FactoryTalk Optix are
      established choices. OpenWebHMI aims to offer small and mid-size plants
      an open-source, self-hosted alternative with open project authoring.
    </p>
    <p>
      Engineers should be able to choose visual editing, terminal tools, or
      help from their own coding agent, while keeping control of what is published.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>v1.0 scope</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Engineering targets, not certified capacity. Workload, hardware, history
      recording rate and client subscriptions determine the supported deployment.
    </p>
    <ul class="scope-list">
      <li><strong>Single gateway</strong> per deployment</li>
      <li><strong>Target: ≤ 50,000</strong> live tags per gateway; validation pending</li>
      <li><strong>Target: 30 days</strong> of selected process history; recording-rate and storage limits apply</li>
      <li><strong>Separate Edge profile</strong> for low-resource headless deployments; validation pending</li>
      <li><strong>Target: ≤ 50</strong> concurrent runtime clients with bounded subscriptions</li>
      <li><strong>Five v1 driver targets:</strong> Rockwell EtherNet/IP, OPC UA, Modbus TCP/RTU, MQTT (incl. Sparkplug B), Beckhoff ADS</li>
      <li><strong>Web-only runtime</strong>; Tauri desktop runtime is post-1.0</li>
      <li><strong>Linux, macOS, Windows</strong> for gateway and designer</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>Stack at a glance</h2>
    <div class="stack-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Layer</th>
            <th>Ignition</th>
            <th>Optix</th>
            <th>OpenWebHMI</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Core / runtime</td>
            <td>Java 17 / JVM</td>
            <td>C++/Qt native platform + C#/.NET NetLogic*</td>
            <td><strong>Rust</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Designer</td>
            <td>Java/Swing</td>
            <td>C++/Qt + web technology; C#/.NET authoring*</td>
            <td><strong>Browser-first React/TypeScript; optional Tauri</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Web HMI</td>
            <td>Perspective — React/TypeScript over Java</td>
            <td>Web Presentation Engine — HTML5/browser</td>
            <td><strong>React + TypeScript</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Scripting</td>
            <td>Jython 2.7.4 — Python 2.7 language level</td>
            <td>C#/.NET NetLogic</td>
            <td><strong>CPython 3.11+</strong> in worker subprocesses</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Open source?</td>
            <td>❌</td>
            <td>❌</td>
            <td>✅ <strong>AGPL core / MPL protocols</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      *Optix is closed source. C++/Qt is supported by Rockwell's native-runtime
      documentation and current ASEM/Rockwell engineering roles, while C#/.NET
      NetLogic is publicly documented. The exact internal boundary between its
      designer and runtime is not public.
    </p>
    <h3>Why this combination matters</h3>
    <ul class="scope-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Rust owns the always-on plant-facing core:</strong> protocol
        drivers, tags, alarms, history, and client fan-out get memory safety and
        a predictable footprint without a managed runtime.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>TypeScript owns the human interface:</strong> the designer,
        browser runtime, and component library share web standards and a broad
        contributor ecosystem instead of maintaining unrelated UI stacks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>CPython owns user automation and data work:</strong> scripts run
        in worker subprocesses with gateway-enforced timeouts. Available
        third-party packages depend on the deployment environment; process
        separation is not a complete security sandbox.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open licensing makes operator control architectural:</strong>
        plants can audit, self-host, air-gap, modify, and retain the system under
        the AGPL core and MPL protocol terms without a per-server license.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The advantage is not that Java or C# are incapable. It is that each
      OpenWebHMI language has one clear job, and the complete platform remains
      inspectable and operator-owned.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/sergiogallegos/OpenWebHMI/blob/main/docs/stack-rationale.md"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >Full reasoning: docs/stack-rationale.md ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>v1.x feature highlights</h2>
    <ul class="scope-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Audit-log 21 CFR Part 11 framing:</strong>
        tamper-evident SHA-256 hash chain, verify CLI, and an engineering
        coverage map with every clause marked partial or out-of-scope.
        <a
          href={`${BLOB}/docs/audit-log-cfr21-mapping.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Read the mapping ↗</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Project themes and Material-inspired demo widgets:</strong>
        light/dark CSS-variable themes plus an opt-in demo pack covering
        representative controls without changing widget prop contracts.
        <a
          href={`${BLOB}/docs/theme-editor.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Theme editor ↗</a>
        <a
          href={`${BLOB}/docs/widget-packs.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Widget packs ↗</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Historian capacity and retention planning:</strong>
        SQLite-backed storage with sizing formulas, online backup/restore, and
        explicit opt-in pruning primitives.
        <a
          href={`${BLOB}/docs/historian.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Historian guide ↗</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Raspberry Pi 4/5 edge deployment guide:</strong>
        native Pi build path, systemd unit, reverse-proxy guidance, and honest
        hardware-smoke boundaries for operator validation.
        <a
          href={`${BLOB}/docs/deployment/raspberry-pi.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Pi deployment ↗</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Widget export/import between projects:</strong>
        human-readable <code>.owhmi-widget</code> files, schema-version checks,
        tag-binding warnings, and regenerated component ids on import.
        <a
          href={`${BLOB}/docs/widget-export-import.md`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Widget transfer ↗</a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>Where we are</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Earlier phase milestones established the source foundation. The current
      hardening and architecture work still needs validation before a public beta.
    </p>
    <ol class="phase-list">
      <li>
        <span class="badge">Source foundation</span>
        Gateway and browser runtime, form-based Designer, history, alarms,
        authentication, scripting, and a simulator-backed Rockwell vertical slice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="badge">Current work</span>
        Security and command correctness, driver integration, reusable engine
        boundaries, browser delivery, safe project publishing, and durable
        history. Capacity, recovery, platform, and hardware evidence remain pending.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="badge">Beta planning horizon</span>
        As of October 2026, allow at least another year before a public beta:
        October 2027 or later, subject to scope and validation. This is a planning
        horizon, not an announced release date. Private demos and feedback can
        happen earlier; production 1.0 has separate validation gates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="badge">After v1.0</span>
        Optional manufacturing modules and edge + cloud services for multiple
        locations are separate future tracks with no committed delivery dates.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/sergiogallegos/OpenWebHMI/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >Detailed roadmap ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container section">
    <h2>What we explicitly don't try to be</h2>
    <ul class="dont-list">
      <li>A hard-real-time control platform.</li>
      <li>A safety-rated (IEC 61508 / SIL) system.</li>
      <li>A replacement for vendor-specific PLC engineering tooling.</li>
      <li>An MES platform — optional manufacturing modules are post-1.0 direction, not a 1.0 promise.</li>
      <li>Loads above the defined 50K-tag / 50-client Medium workload or multi-gateway deployments in v1.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="container section cta">
    <h2>Help shape OpenWebHMI</h2>
    <p>
      Review the plan and progress, then share what you like, what is missing,
      and what you would expect from an HMI/SCADA platform. Describe your use
      case or suggest a feature through GitHub issues. Feedback is welcome
      while the product is still taking shape.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={`${BLOB}/docs/roadmap.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Review the roadmap ↗</a>
      ·
      <a href={`${BLOB}/docs/agents/board.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">See development progress ↗</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Follow Sergio Gallegos on X or visit his personal website for updates
      and follow-up conversations.
    </p>
    <FeedbackLinks />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding: 72px 0 60px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        ellipse at top,
        var(--brand-soft) 0%,
        transparent 60%
      );
  }

  .hero-inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    gap: 20px;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 64ch;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .hero-meta {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .status-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: 24px;
  }

  .status-grid article {
    padding: 22px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  .status-grid h3 {
    margin: 16px 0 8px;
  }

  .status-grid p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .status-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .section {
    padding: 56px 0;
  }

  .scope-list,
  .dont-list,
  .phase-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .scope-list li,
  .dont-list li {
    padding: 12px 16px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .phase-list {
    counter-reset: phase;
  }

  .phase-list li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .phase-list .badge {
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .stack-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .cta {
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--surface);
    margin-top: 32px;
    padding: 56px 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero {
      padding: 48px 0 40px;
    }

    .hero-inner {
      gap: 16px;
    }

    .hero-inner :global(svg) {
      width: 72px;
      height: 72px;
    }

    .lede {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    .hero-actions {
      width: 100%;
      gap: 10px;
    }

    .section {
      padding: 38px 0;
    }

    .phase-list li {
      align-items: flex-start;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 8px;
    }

    .cta {
      margin-top: 16px;
      padding: 36px 16px;
      border-radius: var(--radius);
    }
  }
</style>
